   "pages": [
    {
     "page": 1,
     "text": "**CLINICAL SUMMARY - Mccall-May Medical Group**\n\n**Patient:** Patrick Gomez \n**DOB:** 06/30/1963 \n**Sex:** M \n**MRN:** 949748\n\n---\n\n**Encounters:**\n\n**2024-12-25** \n- **Reason for Visit:** Back strain \n- **Blood Pressure:** 145/89 \n- **Weight:** 197 lbs \n\n**2024-04-07** \n- **Reason for Visit:** Malignant melanoma, stage I \n- **Blood Pressure:** 146/87 \n- **Weight:** 184 lbs \n\n**2023-07-20** \n- **Reason for Visit:** URI \n- **Blood Pressure:** 148/80 \n- **Weight:** 197 lbs \n\n**2022-10-31** \n- **Reason for Visit:** Malignant melanoma, stage I \n- **Blood Pressure:** 148/88 \n- **Weight:** 181 lbs \n\n**2022-02-11** \n- **Reason for Visit:** Malignant melanoma, stage I \n- **Blood Pressure:** 138/86 \n- **Weight:** 185 lbs \n\n**2021-05-24** \n- **Reason for Visit:** Flu shot \n- **Blood Pressure:** 143/85 \n- **Weight:** 188 lbs \n\n---\n\n**Assessment/Plan:**\n\n- **Malignant melanoma, stage I:** This condition, diagnosed in June 2013, has been in controlled following treatment approximately 10.8 years ago. Currently, the patient is not on any medications related to this condition. The melanoma has remained stable and there have been no indications of recurrence. The patient has undergone regular follow-ups and surveillance, which include routine skin examinations and imaging studies as needed. \n\n- **Back strain:** The patient presented with complaints of back strain. Physical examination of the back revealed no abnormalities, and the patient reported improvement with conservative management, including rest and over-the-counter pain relievers. The patient was advised to continue with ergonomic practices at work and to avoid heavy lifting.\n\n- **Upper Respiratory Infection (URI):** During the visit on July 20, 2023, the patient reported symptoms consistent with a URI. The examination revealed normal sinus and throat findings, and no signs of pneumonia or other complications were observed. The patient was advised to stay rest, and use over-the-counter medications for symptom relief.\n\n- **Hypertension:** The patient’s blood pressure readings have been consistently elevated but controlled with lifestyle modifications and, if necessary, medication. The patient was educated on the importance of dietary changes, regular exercise, and weight management to control blood pressure.\n\n- **Obesity:** The patient’s weight has fluctuated between 181 and 197 lbs over the past few years. The importance of maintaining a healthy weight was emphasized, and the patient was referred to a nutritionist for dietary guidance.\n\n- **Tobacco Use:** The patie"
    },
    {
     "page": 2,
     "text": "nt a history of tobacco use, having smoked 10 cigarettes per day starting in March 1995 and quitting in January 2021. This information is relevant as tobacco use is a risk factor for several health conditions, including cardiovascular disease and various cancers.\n\n- **Alcohol Use:** The patient reports consuming approximately one alcoholic drink per week. This moderate alcohol consumption does not appear to pose a significant health risk at this level.\n\n---\n\n**Vital Signs (at various visits):**\n\n- **Blood Pressure:** \n - 2024-12-25: 145/89\n - 2024-04-07: 146/87\n - 2023-07-20: 148/80\n - 2022-10-31: 148/88\n - 2022-02-11: 138/86\n - 2021-05-24: 143/85\n\n- **Weight (lbs):** \n - 2024-12-25: 197\n - 2024-04-07: 184\n - 2023-07-20: 197\n - 2022-10-31: 181\n - 2022-02-11: 185\n - 2021-05-24: 188\n\n---\n\n**Social History:**\n\n- **Tobacco Use:** Former smoker, 10 cigarettes per day, quit in January 2021.\n- **Alcohol Use:** Approximately one drink per week.\n- **Family History:** The patient has a family history of significant conditions, including breast cancer in the mother, who was diagnosed at age 46 and passed away at age 52. The father had a stroke and passed away at age 55. The patient's mother is alive and has type 2 diabetes, diagnosed at age 66.\n\n---\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\nPatrick Gomez has been under careful surveillance for his stage I malignant melanoma, which remains controlled. He continues to manage hypertension and obesity with lifestyle modifications. The patient has a history of tobacco use but has successfully quit. Alcohol consumption is within moderate limits. The patient's family history includes significant conditions, which underscores the importance of continued monitoring and preventive care.\n"
